#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "8cc1617d-ba3f-5ae3-aca0-90354ffbd62f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4273" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid \\mathbf{a}|=11$. The vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid \\mathbf{b}|=8$. Find the exact value of $\\mid \\mathbf{a}+\\textbf{b} \\mid$." :question/worked-solution "We use the triangle law of addition to find the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/a1/a122c2a1146f27a089f991f7e0bd07e4180ca4622bfc95a6b85afc83f339c11d.png)\n\nNotice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 11^{2} + 8^{2} \\\\\n&= 185\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a}+\\textbf{b} &= \\sqrt{185}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c145c97c-c313-597f-b84e-8adb3bdf9c14" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Law of Cosines"}] :question/id #uuid "f0c94cce-790d-50b0-bd9b-13b64867848b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4275" :question/problem "Consider the diagram shown above. If $\\mid \\mathbf{a}|=4$ and $\\mid \\mathbf{b}|=3$, Find the exact value of$\\mid \\mathbf{a-b} \\mid$.\n\n![](images/92/922fa6572855e2a40f2b0fdd97bbbc5d6ddf2323228d99d5ab74a7aa7c2072f8.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can interpret the subtraction\n$\\mid \\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} \\mid$\nas the addition\n$\\mid \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b}) \\mid$.\n\nThe vector $-\\mathbf{b}$ has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ and the direction that is opposite to $\\mathbf{b}$. We use the triangle law of addition to find $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\mathbf{b}$. The resultant goes from the start of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\mathbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b1cd51e96439a8e3317fec12c9d1e57aa06bc9a99680849990e853d3ce6dbf7.png)\n\nSo, we can use the law of cosines as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a - b & ^{2} &= \\end{vmatrix}a + \\left.(-b)\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}a & ^{2} + & -b & ^{2} - 2 \\cdot & a & \\cdot & -b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos (180^{∘} - 120^{∘}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}a & ^{2} + & b & ^{2} - 2 & a\\end{vmatrix} \\mid b \\mid \\cos (60^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 3^{2} + 4^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 9 + 16 - 12 \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a-b} &= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0146b8-421b-5d63-a933-3de9bcaa7ab2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Magnitude of a Vector From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ba160db3-7799-5a25-8c50-5147100f7475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5013" :question/problem "Consider the picture shown below. Find the exact value of $\\mid \\mathbf{a} \\mid$.\n\n![](images/14/140b4f8ced622eaf505d97c0f4d9c029365536927ea0c3e38e76904cba84ace2.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1f/1fc7b5efcaa81dd1a3416757c976e5d17b96f5040069b5eeeb941b1f7c149c34.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\nAH=35\\:\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBH=25\\:\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nSo, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{35^{2} + 25^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1850} \\\\\n&= 5\\sqrt{74}mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
